In recent studies, the main group elements exhibited considerable performance as NRR catalysts due to their effectiveness in suppressing hydrogen evolution, which may originate from the intrinsic affinity of the metal v band to the \(\mathrm{N}\,2p\) orbital of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) [33]. For example, Fu et al. first developed an Al-based porous MOF, MIL-100 (Al), for artificial \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) fixation through electrocatalysis (Fig. 3a) [56]. Because of the strong interaction between the \(3p\) band of Al and the \(2p\) orbital of N, MIL-100 (Al) owns excellent ability for \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) sorption, presenting remarkable performance in generating \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) with \(10.6\ \upmu\mathrm{g\ h^{-1}\ cm^{-2}\ m g_{c a t.}}^{-1}\) yield rate and FE of \(22.6\%\) at an ultralow overpotential in \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\)-saturated alkaline solution (Fig. 3c). Notably, the synergistic effect of the skeleton and Al nodes of MIL-100(Al) was further revealed by comparative experiments (Fig. 3b). The moderated catalytic performance was observed in the NRR process catalyzed
by MIL-53(Al) and defective MIL-100(Al). Furthermore, modified with stable superoxide radicals \(\left(\mathbf{O}_{2}\right)\), a defective Zr-MOF \(\mathrm{(UiO-66-NH_{2})}\) was applied for electrosynthesis of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) from \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) as a superior active catalyst [57]. In \(0.1\mathrm{~M~}\) \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\), the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) was generated at a high yield rate of \(52.81\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{h}^{-1}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\,\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat.}}^{-1}\) with \(85.21\%\) FE at \(-0.39\,\mathrm{V}\) versus RHE. The neighboring exposed \(Z\mathbf{r}\) atoms (Zr-OO· and \(Z\mathbf{r}\) site) contribute to the incredible catalytic performance through their synergy effect. Besides, the proton adsorption was unfavored by the exposed \(Z\mathbf{r}\) atoms, which further constrained the hydrogen evolution and hydrazine formation.
In addition to functionalized MOFs, the in situ generated MOFs on commercially available substrates also presented a noteworthy performance in electrochemical \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\)-to- \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) conversion. For example, Liu et al. constructed several self-support heterogeneous catalysts through in situ growth of MOFs on the copper mesh, including \(\mathrm{Cu@Cu-MOF}\) and \(\mathrm{Cu@Ce-MOF-}n\) \((n\!=\!1,\,2\), and 3) (Fig. 4a) [58]. After a thorough examination, the \(\mathrm{Cu@Ce-MOF-2}\) rise above others, and an
\(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield rate of \(14.83\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{h}^{-1}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) with a \(10.81\%\) FE was obtained at \(-0.2\mathrm{~V~}\) versus RHE in \(0.1\mathrm{~M~}\) KOH (Fig. 4b, c). The Ce-unsaturated coordination structure on the surface of Ce-MOF provides oxygen vacancies and serves as active sites for the adsorption and activation of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\), and the conductive copper mesh promotes sufficient charge transfer, making \(\mathrm{Cu@Ce-MOF-2}\) an efficient and stable electrocatalyst. However, the poorly stable Ce-MOF reconstructed into \(\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\) without oxygen vacancies after use.
As an emerging MOF type, two-dimensional (2D) MOF nanosheets show superior catalytic performance due to increased exposed catalytic site, enlarged aspect ratio, enhanced permeability, and improved mass transport. Particularly, Sun and co-workers constructed an ultrathin 2D In-MOF with a thickness of \(1.3\ \mathrm{nm}\) for the exploration of catalytic performance in NRR at different \(\mathrm{pH}\) values (1, 7, and 12) (Fig. 4d) [59]. In an alkaline electrolytic solution \((\mathrm{pH}\!=\!12)\), the maximum \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield of \(79.20\,\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{h}^{-1}\,\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat.}}^{-1}\) was achieved with a \(14.98\%\) FE, while moderate results were
obtained in acidic and neutral media (Fig. 4e). In-MOFs possess a rigid architecture and exhibit excellent stability at all \(\mathrm{pH}\) levels.
As superior molecular catalysts, metalloporphyrin, and derivatives were commonly adopted as catalytic centers for diverse reactions. Cong et al. installed metalloporphyrin (M-TCPP) motifs into MOFs by assembling with \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\), constructing thin M-TCPP nanosheets for \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) production (Fig. 5a) [60]. The MOFs with different metal sites were optimized, and Fe-TCPP exhibited a particularly outstanding performance for generating \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\). The maximum \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield rate \((44.77\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{h}^{-1}\;\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat}}^{-1})\) and FE \((16.23\%)\) were provided at \(-0.3\;\mathrm{V}\) versus RHE in \(0.1\;\mathrm{M}\) HCl (Fig. 5b, c). This work
integrated the merit of molecular catalysts and 2D nanosheets for promoting the electrochemical conversion of the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) to \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) process. Moreover, another ferriporphyrin-based MOF, PCN-222(Fe) was also synthesized and decorated with hydrophobic molecules, organic phosphoric acids (OPA), for electrocatalytic NRR in \(0.1\;\mathrm{M}\) HCl (Fig. 5d) [61]. Surface hydrophobicity modification is a viable approach to restrain HER for accelerating \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\)-to-\(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) fixation (Fig. 5e). Therefore, the as-fabricated OPA-PCN-222(Fe) demonstrated the generation of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) with \(49.7\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{h}^{-1}\;\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat}}^{-1}\) yield rate and \(17.2\%\) FE at \(-0.5\) and \(-0.4\;\mathrm{V}\) versus RHE, respectively (Fig. 5f). Such catalytic performance was significantly superior to that of PCN-222, Fe-TCPP, and PCN-222(Fe) (Fig. 5g).
## 3.1.2 Bimetallic MOFs
Recently, it has been shown that the NRR activity of MOF coordinated with only one metal site is relatively insufficient, and the performance of NRR is expected to improve with the introduction of additional metals to tailor the electronic structures. More active sites can also be created. The strong synergistic effect between bimetallic MOF makes it one of the most active catalysts in the field of electrocatalytic NRR. For example, Duan et al. prepared a zero-dimensional (0D) bimetallic nickel, iron-MOF with expanded porosity and the diminished particle size facilitating the mass and charge transport, which further improved the NRR activity (Fig. 6a) [62]. The DFT calculation emphasized that the iron-dopant minimized the Gibbs free energy in the final cleavage of the \(\mathrm{N-N}\) bond, affording a FE of \(11.5\%\) and an ammonia yield rate of \(9.3\mathrm{~mg~h^{-1}~m g_{cat.}}^{-1}\) at \(-345~\mathrm{mV}\) versus RHE (Fig. 6b, c). Reducing the particle size to expose more active metal sites, increasing mesoporosity to accelerate the mass transport, and modifying the electronic structure to lower the energy barriers for the NRR process, are very efficient to boost the electrocatalytic performance. Additionally, in order to improve the conductivity of MOF, Li et al. developed a 2D bimetallic \(\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Fe}\) MOF nanosheet for the electrosynthesis of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) (Fig. 6d) [63]. After extensive research, the as-fabricated MOF nanosheet meets the requirements of
both OER and NRR, and \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{Fe}\) MOF exhibited the best catalytic performance beyond others (Fig. 6e-h). With the aid of this bifunctional electrode, the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) was generated with a remarkable FE up to \(25.64\%\) , and the corresponding \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield rate of \(8.79\,\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{h}^{-1}\,\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat.}}^{-1}\) at \(-0.2\,\mathrm{V}\) versus RHE.
## 3.1.3 MOF Hybrid Composites
In the field of electrocatalyst development for the NRR process, noble metal nanoparticles have already demonstrated their high efficiency in the adsorption and activation of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) molecules. However, the catalytic robustness always inclines to decrease dramatically attributed to the intrinsic weakness of the metal nanoparticles, which is prone to agglomeration as the reaction proceeds. Developing a proper strategy to well-disperse the metal nanoparticles and alleviate their tendency to agglomerate is an effective way for promoting catalytic efficiency. With rigid skeleton and well-defined pores, MOFs are a suitable substrate for catalytic active metal nanoparticles. Encapsulated in the channels or cavities of MOF structure, the metal nanoparticles are confined against the gathering. Moreover, the residue pores of the composites facilitate the mass transfer, while the competing HER process was restrained by the native hydrophobicity of MOF. Therefore, impregnation with metal nanoparticles can improve the catalytic performance of MOFs
in the electrochemical \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) fixation process. For example, Yang et al. designed a composite of nanoporous gold and ZIF-8 (NPG@ZIF-8) with a core–shell structure (Fig. 7a, b) [64]. As the hydrophobicity of ZIF-8 reduces the proximity of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) molecules to the catalyst, the ammonia yield and FE of NPG@ZIF-8 are significantly increased. At the same time, the micropores inherent in the ZIF structure can adsorb nitrogen on the surface of the catalyst. The core–shell structured metal nanoparticle@MOF composites are promising catalysts for the electrosynthesis of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) under ambient
conditions. Furthermore, Lv et al. [65] prepared an Au-Cu alloy nanoparticle that could also facilitate electron transport between reactants and catalyst surfaces by introducing ZIF-8 to improve the limited specific surface area and exposed active sites (Fig. 7c-h). This enabled the catalyst to achieve an unprecedented ammonia yield of \(23.3\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}\ \mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat}}^{-1}\) with nitrogen and air as feedstocks and acidic electrolytic solutions in all \(\mathrm{pH}\) ranges. Moreover, He and co-workers developed a hydrophobic Au@MOF coated with organosilicon (HT-Au@MOF) for \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) generation [66]. The Au
nanoparticles were enveloped in a thiol-equipped ZIF-8 skeleton through coordination interaction (Fig. 7i, j). In \(0.1\mathrm{~M~}\) \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) , the highest \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield rate of \(49.5~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat.}}^{-1}\) and FE of \(60.9\%\) were realized at \(-\,0.3\,\mathrm{~V~}\) versus RHE (Fig. 7k). In the NRR process, the HER was significantly suppressed by the hydrophobicity from the organosilicon layer, and outstanding catalytic properties were achieved attributed to the active Au catalytic site as well as the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) aggregation effect of the porous ZIF-8 (Fig. 7l, m). More recently, Wen and co-workers fabricated a UiO-66-based composite with electron-rich PdCu nanoparticles implanted into the cavity and hydrophobic polydimethylsiloxane coating \((\mathrm{PdCu@UiO-S@PDMS})\) [67]. The as-prepared MOFhybrid was adopted as an efficient NRR electrocatalyst for the generation of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) . Due to the integration of hydrophobicity, proton supply, and catalytic activity from each
component of the \(\mathrm{PdCu@UiO-S@PDMS}\) , the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) was successively produced with \(20.24\,\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{h}^{-1}\,\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat.}}^{-1}\) yield rate and \(13.16\%\) FE at \(-\,0.25\;\mathrm{V}\) versus RHE in \(0.1\;\mathrm{M}\) HCl.
In addition to the aqueous electrolytic solutions, the NRR conducted in organic solutions to bypass HER also exhibited superior performance with heterogeneous catalysts. For instance, Lee and colleagues deposited ZIF-71 on an Ag-Au platform using a wet chemical deposition method, fabricating a ZIF-coated Ag-Au electrode \((\mathrm{Ag-Au}@Z\mathrm{IF})\) for \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) electrosynthesis in dry THF solution with \(\mathrm{LiCF}_{3}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\) as the electrolyte (Fig. 8a) [68]. The significant suppression of competing water electrolysis was realized attributed to the water-repelling effect of the ZIF layer, and the enhanced catalytic performance was achieved due to the porous structure of ZIF that increases the concentration of nitrogen gas and prevents agglomeration of Ag-Au nanoparticles.
Continuously, two similar works were proposed by the same group. For the electrocatalytic fixation of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) to \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) in THF media, a ZIF-encapsulated \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Au}\) electrocatalyst has been synthesized and the d-band electronic structure of the bimetallic alloy has been modified, reducing the surface adsorption of a hydrogen atom to improve the NRR performance (Fig. 8b) [69]. In dry THF media, the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) to \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) process catalyzed by \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Au}@Z\mathrm{IF}\) achieves up to 4 times more FE than bare \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Au}\), obtaining a high FE of \(>44\%\) and an \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield rate of \(>161~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat.}}^{-1}\) under ambient conditions. After experimental comparison and theoretical calculation, the originally unfavored \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) reduction process becomes favored due to the hydrophobic surface, concentrating effect, and electronic modification caused by the ZIF deposit. In another example, the authors confined metallic electrocatalysts in the ZIF-71 structure, fabricating the \(\mathbf{M}(\varpi Z\mathrm{IF}\) platform for high-selective \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) production (Fig. 8c)
[70]. Then, the hydrophobic functionalized oleyamine was installed superficially for water exclusion, and butanol was impregnated into the ZIF structure as a proton supplier. The performance of electrocatalytic \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) fixation was examined in a dry THF electrocatalytic solution. Notably, different from the other two works, the catalytic efficiency can be affected by the water content, which gradually increases in the interval \(0{-}0.1\%\) v/v and then decreases with the further growth of water. These works enriched the design and synthesis of proper electrocatalysts as well as the choice of electrolyte solutions for electrocatalytic \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) synthesis.
Recently, thin-layered 2D nanomaterials exhibited their superior catalytic performance in the electrochemical conversion of simple molecules [71]. After the hybridization with MOFs, these nanocomposites can elevate the product selectivity and further enhance the catalytic efficiency. For example, Liang et al. prepared ZIF-67 on the \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\) layer by
in situ growth method for \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) synthesis (Fig. 9a, b) [72]. By virtue of the high porosity and large specific surface area of ZIF as well as the excellent electrical conductivity of 2D \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\), the composite material ZIF-67@Ti3C2 exhibited excellent \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield of \(6.52\;\upmu\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{h}^{-1}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) and FE of \(20.2\%\) at \(-0.4\;\mathrm{V}\) versus RHE in \(0.1\;\mathrm{M}\) KOH. Notably, due to the synergistic effect of ZIF-67 and \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\), the electrocatalytic activity of composite ZIF-67@Ti3C2 for \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) fixation is significantly higher than individual components, ZIF-67 and \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\) (Fig. 9c). In another work, Xu et al. developed a MIL-101(Fe) modified \(\mathrm{MoS}_{3}\) nanocomposite (MIL-101(Fe)/ \(\mathrm{MoS}_{3}\)) for artificial \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) fixation (Fig. 9d) [73]. The hybrid material possesses a homogeneous nanolayer of \(\mathrm{MoS}_{3}\) and crystal nanodots of MIL-101(Fe) (Fig. 9e, f). In \(0.1\;\mathrm{M}\) HCl, the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) transformation from \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) was achieved with \(25.7\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{h}^{-1}\;\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat.}}^{-1}\) yield rate and \(36.71\%\) FE at \(-0.1\;\mathrm{V}\) versus RHE (Fig. 9g). Similarly, Duan and co-workers developed ZIF-71 enveloped ball-like \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}\) nanoflowers \((\mathrm{MoS}_{2}@\) ZIF-71) via layer-by-layer growth strategy for electrocatalytic NRR application (Fig. 9h) [74]. Catalyzed by \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}/\) CP electrode coated with ZIF-71, the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) products were
provided in a maximum of \(56.69\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{h}^{-1}\;\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat.}}^{-1}\) yield rate and \(30.91\%\) FE at \(-0.2\;\mathrm{V}\) versus RHE in \(0.1\;\mathrm{M}\) \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) (Fig. 9i, j). The hydrophobicity of porous ZIF-71 is responsible to concentrate \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and suppress HER, and the synergistic effect between \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}\) and ZIF-71 was intensively confirmed by the control experiments (Fig. 9k). These aforementioned advances put forward innovative strategies for fabricating hybrid composites of 2D nanostructure and MOF, and expanded the choices of promising candidates of electrocatalysts for the NRR process.
Furthermore, the carbon-based catalysts containing defects present a positive effect in adsorbing and activating \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) molecules, thus improving the process of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) fixation. The integration of conductivity and catalytic activity of carbon nanotubes (CNTs) and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) enrichment and hydrophobicity of MOFs make the \(\mathrm{CNT}@\) MOF composites promising NRR electrocatalysts for practical use. Lv et al. proposed a combined catalytic method of MOF doping CNT or N-doped CNT (NCNT) for electrochemical \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) fixation [75]. The MOF dopant enlarged the water contact angle and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) sorption uptake of the material, inferring improved
water-repelling ability for rationally restraining HER and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) enrichment for promoted \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) fixation.
Based on the aforementioned works listed in Table 1, pristine MOFs and MOF-hybrid materials are certificated as promising electrocatalysts for efficient artificial \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) fixation for \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\). In the framework, the metals are generally considered to have the ability to adsorb and activate \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) molecules, while hydrophobic organic linkages are supposed to suppress the competing HER process. Meanwhile, the porous skeletons not only provide sufficient space to accelerate the reduction of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) but also offer the opportunity to couple with other functional materials, which facilitates the integration and optimization for better catalytic performance. Additionally, conductive MOFs and 2D MOF nanosheets exhibit their unique promotion effect for electrocatalytic \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\)-to-\(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\), such as enhanced conductivity, more exposed active sites, and more rapid mass transfer, etc.
## 3.2 MOF-derived Electrocatalysts
MOFs can also be used as pre-catalysts. Through proper pyrolysis or calcination processes, the coordinatively fabricated MOF can be derived into carbon-based nanomaterials with enhanced conductivity and stability, thus further improving the electrocatalytic efficiency. In most cases, the framework can be reserved or slightly shrank with organic linkage decomposed into carbon materials, while the metal residues were converted into metal oxides, metal sulfides, metal carbides, phosphating compounds, selenides, and even single metal atoms as catalytic active sites that were firmly installed on the carbon-based materials. The following contents are mainly focused on the MOF-derived electrocatalysts for NRR, such as porous carbon catalysts, single-atom catalysts, and other nanostructured composites.
## 3.2.1 Heteroatom-doped Porous Carbon Catalysts
3.2.1 Heteroatom-doped Porous Carbon CatalystsBy virtue of abundant origins, fabricable structure, and tunable function, non-metal catalysts have become more and more attractive in various electrochemical transformations [76]. Among the reported non-metal catalysts, MOF-derived carbons received much attention due to their merits of high surface area, adjustable porosity, and excellent thermal stability. Most importantly, changing the pyrolysis temperature can adjust the degree of graphitization, which can change the electronic and geometric structure of carbon, and thus improve the performance of NRR. For example, Liu et al. prepared a cost-effective N-doped porous carbon (NPC) by carbonized ZIF-8 and utilized it as electrocatalysts for \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) synthesis (Fig. 10a-c) [77]. The \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption was facilitated by the nitrogen content, and pyridinic and pyrrolic nitrogen were verified as active sites for the fixation of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\). Compared with transition metals, metal-free MOF-derived N-doped carbon significantly inhibited HER activity in the acidic media \((0.05\;\mathrm{M}\;\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4})\), thus promoting the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield reached \(1.40\;\mathrm{mmol}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\;\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) at \(-0.9\;\mathrm{V}\) versus RHE. Moreover, similar work was also reported by Mukherjee and colleagues that the ZIF-derived carbon catalyst exhibited
encouraging activity and stability for electrochemical \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) conversion in alkaline media (Fig. 10d-g) [78]. The C-ZIF-1100-1 was prepared from the pyrolysis of ZIF-8 precursor at \(1100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(1\,\mathrm{h}\), providing the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) with FE up to \(10.2\%\) at \(-0.3\;\mathrm{V}\) versus RHE \(0.1\;\mathrm{M}\) KOH. Intriguingly, the \(\mathrm{N}_{3}\) site of N-doped carbon formed during high-temperature pyrolysis was claimed to be the catalytic active center for the electrochemical reduction of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\). Both examples indicated the ZIF-derived NPC being a low-cost metal-free catalyst for \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) generation via \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) reduction.
In addition to the N-dopants, the NPC materials can be further activated by the introduction of other heteroatoms, providing more active sites for adsorption and reduction of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) molecules. For example, Song et al. prepared N, P co-doped porous carbon electrocatalysts from MOF precursors [79]. Through the carbonization of MOF-5, dicyandiamide, and triphenylphosphine mixture at \(900\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), NP-C-MOF-5 was fabricated and adopted as the electrocatalyst for electrocatalytic \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) fixation (Fig. 10h-k). In acidic electrolytic solutions, the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) was obtained with the yields of \(1.08\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{h}^{-1}\;\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat.}}^{-1}\). As another example, Wang et al. prepared sulfur-modified N-MPC from metal azolate frameworks (MAFs) for electrocatalysis of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) conversion [80].
The morphology and structure of MAF were maintained in the pyrolyzed N-MPC, and the introduction of sulfur provided an active site for \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption and subsequent reduction (Fig. 10l). Experimental results show that S/NMPC exhibited excellent \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) -to- \(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) selectivity and electrochemical stability, offering the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield of \(45.51~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) \(\mathrm{mg_{cat}}^{-1}\) and FE of \(25.16\%\) at \(-0.3\) and \(-0.2\;\mathrm{V}\) versus RHE, respectively (Fig. 10m). Both examples demonstrated that the incorporation of heteroatoms into carbon-based electrocatalysts can improve NRR performance.
## 3.2.2  Single-atom Catalysts
Single-atom catalysts (SACs) are widely used in various electrocatalytic reduction processes because of their unique electronic structure, uniform low coordination environment, atomically dispersed active sites, and maximum atomic utilization [28]. In general, MOF-derived SAC catalysts were constructed through the pyrolysis or carbonization of MOF precursors with metal embedded or installed. The highly dispersive metal atoms can be stabilized by the defect-rich supporting carbon substrates. With the merits of electric conductivity, well-defined porous structure, and quasi-molecular catalytic activity, the as-prepared SAC catalysts presented enhanced performance of electrochemical NRR.
Among the many precious metal-based SACs, Ru-based SAC is located at the top of the volcano map, providing more opportunities for NRR. Geng et al. synthesized the nitrogendoped carbon with dispersive Ru single atoms (Ru SAs/N–C) via pyrolysis of ZIF-8 with Ru-dopants and used them as electrocatalysts for the NRR process (Fig. 11a, b) [81]. At \(-0.2\;\mathrm{V}\) versus RHE, the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) was produced with an FE of \(29.6\%\) and a record-breaking yield of \(120.9\,\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{h}^{-1}\,\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat}}^{-1}\) , still the top-ranked \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield so far (Fig. 11c,d). Furthermore, Tao et al. developed a \(\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}\) -doped carbon composite catalyst with single Ru sites \((\mathrm{Ru}@Z\mathrm{rO}_{2}/\mathrm{NC})\) for the electrosynthesis of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) from \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) [82]. Ru ions were encapsulated in UiO-66 by a hydrothermal method and then annealed to generate the \(\mathrm{Ru}@Z\mathrm{rO}_{2}/\mathrm{NC}\) in \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) . HAADFSTEM observations confirmed the distribution of single Ru sites on the carbon support, the size of most of the Ru atoms fell in the range of \(0.1{-}0.2\;\mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. 11e). The formation of atom-dispersed Ru is most likely due to uncoordinated - \(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) groups stabilizing the precursor and inhibiting Ru aggregation during pyrolysis. At a more negative than \(-0.31\mathrm{~V~}\)
versus RHE, the FE of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) approaches 0 due to competitive HER for \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{NC}\) , whereas the addition of \(Z\mathrm{rO}_{2}\) significantly promoted the generation of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) at all applied potentials. Catalyzed by the \(\mathrm{Ru}@Z\mathrm{rO}_{2}/\mathrm{NC}\) , the FE of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) reaches a maximum of about \(21\%\) at \(-0.11\mathrm{~V~}\) versus RHE and the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield of \(3.665\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat}}.^{-1}\) at \(-0.21\;\mathrm{V}\) versus RHE (Fig. 11f, g).
versus RHE, the FE of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) approaches 0 due to competitive HER for \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{NC}\), whereas the addition of \(\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}\) significantly promoted the generation of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) at all applied potentials. Catalyzed by the \(\mathrm{Ru}@Z\mathrm{rO}_{2}/\mathrm{NC}\), the FE of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) reaches a maximum of about \(21\%\) at \(-0.11\mathrm{~V~}\) versus RHE and the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield of \(3.665\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat}}.^{-1}\) at \(-0.21\;\mathrm{V}\) versus RHE (Fig. 11f, g).In addition to Ru SACs, the transition-metal SACs were also demonstrated as effective catalysts for the electrochemical artificial fixation of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\). For example, Lü et al. fabricated N-doped carbon frameworks anchored with isolated Fe single atoms (ISAS-Fe/NC) through carbonization and etching of pre-synthesized bimetallic Fe-doped ZIF-8 (Fig. 12a, b) [83]. The as-prepared Fe SAC exhibited superior catalytic performance for the generation of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\), with the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield of \(62.9\pm2.7~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat}}.^{-1}\) and FE of \(18.6\pm0.8\%\) at \(-0.4\;\mathrm{V}\) versus RHE in neutral media (Fig. 12c, d). The electrocatalytic stability of ISAS-Fe/NC was verified by a long-term electrolysis test, with slight fluctuation of current density in \(24\,\mathrm{{h}}\) (Fig. 12e). This work shed light on the practical use of low-cost transition-metal SACs in environmentally friendly \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) synthesis with low energy consumption. Furthermore, the Fe SACs were also developed from the pyrolysis of PCN-222(Fe) precursors \((\mathrm{Fe}_{1}{-}\mathrm{N}{-}\mathrm{C})\), reported by Zhang and co-workers (Fig. 12f) [84]. The nanorod shape of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{1}{-}\mathrm{N}{-}\mathrm{C}\) was inherited from the MOF precursor, and the atomically dispersed Fe sites were confirmed by the HAADF-STEM image (Fig. 12g-i). At a relatively low overpotential, \(-0.05\mathrm{~V~}\) versus RHE, the maximum \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield reached \(1.56\!\times\!10^{-11}\,\mathrm{mol\,cm}^{-2}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) with \(4.51\%\) FE (Fig. 12j). In another example, Liu et al. prepared a Fe–N/C SAC from the pyrolysis of Fe-TPP/ZIF-8 precursors (Fig. 12k, l) [85]. Obtained through ball milling, the precursors were annealed and subsequently fixed on pre-treated carbon papers by Nafion. Then, the electrosynthesis of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) was mediated by the as-fabricated Fe–N/C-CP catalytic electrode, realizing the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield of \(2.27~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat}}.^{-1}\) with \(7.67\%\) FE at \(-0.2\;\mathrm{V}\) versus RHE (Fig. 12m).Apart from Fe, advances using other transition-metal-based SACs were also proposed for the investigation of electrocatalysts in NRR. For instance, Liu et al. prepared Co- single atom embedded N-doped porous carbon (CSA/NPC) by annealing the Co-doped ZIF-8 (Fig. 13a) [86]. The polyhedral crystal morphology of ZIF can also be observed in the CSA/NPC (Fig. 13b-d). And the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield and FE were \(0.86\,\upmu\mathrm{mol}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\,\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) and \(10.5\%\) , respectively (Fig. 13e).
Apart from Fe, advances using other transition-metalbased SACs were also proposed for the investigation of electrocatalysts in NRR. For instance, Liu et al. prepared Co- single atom embedded N-doped porous carbon (CSA/ NPC) by annealing the Co-doped ZIF-8 (Fig. 13a) [86]. The polyhedral crystal morphology of ZIF can also be observed in the CSA/NPC (Fig. 13b-d). And the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield and FE were \(0.86\,\upmu\mathrm{mol}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\,\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) and \(10.5\%\) , respectively (Fig. 13e).
The as-prepared CSA/NPC not only has high NRR activity and selectivity but also has good electrochemical stability (Fig. 13f). Furthermore, Gao et al. synthesized a cobalt/ nitrogen-doped porous carbon (Co/NC) electrocatalyst consisting of a single Co site by carbonizing ZIF-67. In \(0.1\mathrm{~M~}\) KOH, the maximum \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield was \(5.1~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat}}.^{-1}\) at \(-\,0.4\mathrm{~V~}\) versus RHE, and the FE reached up to \(10.1\%\) at \(-\,0.1\mathrm{~V~}\) versus RHE [87]. In another case, Mukherjee and colleagues prepared an atomically dispersed Ni site electrocatalyst through the pyrolysis of the Ni-Zn bimetallic organic framework (Fig. 13g) [88]. With the partial replacement of \(Z\mathfrak{n}\) nodes by Ni during the formation process, the Ni- \(\cdot\mathrm{N}_{x}\mathrm{C}\) coordination structure was formed after annealing
(Fig. 13h). Notably, the formation of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) was not limited by the \(\mathrm{pH}\) of the electrolytic solution, yielding the optimized \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield of \(115\,\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\,\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) at \(-\,0.8\;\mathrm{V}\) versus RHE under neutral conditions, and a high FE of \(22.9\%\) at \(-\,0.2\,\mathrm{V}\) versus RHE under alkaline conditions (Fig. 13i-k).
Compared with precious metals, low-cost semi-metals are also listed as candidate materials for NRR, especially nanoscale Bi. Bi-based NRR electrocatalysts with ideal electronic structure and high-exposure active sites can be prepared through reasonable composite design. Xi et al. prepared hollow titanium nitride nanorods bound by monoatomic Bi and fixed them in NC loaded on carbon cloth (NC/ Bi SAs/TiN/CC) (Fig. 13l) [89]. The composite exhibits a
superior \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield rate of \(76.15~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat.}}^{-1}\) at \(-0.8\;\mathrm{V}\) versus RHE and a high FE of \(24.60\%\) at \(-0.5\:\mathrm{V}\) versus RHE in \(0.1\;\mathrm{M}\,\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) solution (Fig. 13n). In addition, the synergistic effect of Bi-SAs and TiN can simultaneously promote \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) hydrogenation and inhibit HER (Fig. 13n).
Based on the above advances in electrochemical NRR promoted by the MOF-derived SACs, the precisely designed synthetic route of electrocatalysts play a vital role in enhancing energy conversion and \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) generation. Thanks to the N-doped porous carbon substrates, the electric conductivity, proton transportation, and stabilization of single metal atoms
were guaranteed, thus providing superior catalytic performance for artificial \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) fixation.
## 3.2.3 Other MOF-Derived Nanostructures
Apart from the metal SACs, other MOF-derived nanostructures by high-temperature pyrolysis were also proved to be effective electrocatalysts in NRR for \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) synthesis, including the carbon materials that dispersed by metal nanoparticles, metal oxides, metal sulfides, and metal phosphides. In general, the morphology of MOF precursors can be maintained
after the pyrolysis, yielding porous carbon materials with dispersive metal sites. For example, Zhang et al. developed a rhombic shape Ru NPs dispersed N-doped carbon framework (NC@Ru) by carbonization of Ru-doped ZIF-8 at high
temperatures (Fig. 14a) [90]. According to the comparison of NRR performance, \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Ru}{-5}\) was verified to be one of the optimal catalyst, providing the highest yield of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) is \(16.68\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat.}}\) . \({}^{-1}\) at \(-0.4\:\mathrm{V}\) versus RHE and that of FE
is \(14.23\%\) at \(-0.3\;\mathrm{V}\) vs RHE in \(0.1\,\mathrm{M}\) KOH electrolytic solution (Fig. 14b, c). Meanwhile, without the introduction of external metal sources, the \(\mathrm{Co/C-900}\) composite was developed by Liu et al. through the direct calcination of ZIF-67 at \(900\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(1\,\mathrm{h}\) in \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) (Fig. 14d) [91]. The Co nodes in ZIF-67 were fully adapted to the \(\mathrm{Co/C-900}\) as catalytic active sites. The \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) reduction was measured using \(\mathrm{Co/C-900}\) as the catalysts in \(0.1\,\mathrm{M}\) KOH, displaying the highest FE of \(11.53\%\) and the maximum \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) production rate of \(4.66\,\upmu\mathrm{mol}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\,\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) at \(-0.3\;\mathrm{V}\) versus RHE. Similarly, another MOF-derived Co-based nanomaterial was also proposed by Yin et al. [92]. The \(\mathrm{Co@N}\) -doped carbon materials \(\mathrm{(Co@NC)}\) were fabricated by ZIF-67 precursor annealing at high temperatures. Under ambient conditions, \(\mathrm{Co@NC}\) exhibits excellent electrocatalytic performance. At \(-0.9\mathrm{~V~}\) versus \(\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}\) , the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) was offered an \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield of \(1.57\times10^{-10}\;\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) with FE up to \(21.79\%\) . Furthermore, Wang et al. synthesized an electrocatalyst at the Fe-N3 site using Fe-ZIF-CNT composites as templates (Fig. 14e) [93]. In \(0.1\,\mathrm{M}\) KOH media, this catalyst exhibits enhanced NRR activity with \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) production of \(34.83~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat}}^{-1}\) , and FE of \(9.28\%\) at \(-0.2\;\mathrm{V}\)
versus RHE. In 2021, Wang et al. developed an efficient electrocatalyst for the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) -to- \(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) process by confining Bi NPs into carbon rods (CRs) (Bi NPs@CRs) through annealing of Bi-MOF \(@\) CRs (Fig. 14f) [94]. In \(0.1\;\mathrm{M}\;\mathrm{HCl}\) , a high FE of \(11.5\%\) and a large \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield of \(20.8~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat}}^{-1}\) for Bi NPs@CRs were obtained at \(-0.55\:\mathrm{V}\) and \(-0.6\;\mathrm{V}\) versus RHE, respectively (Fig. 14g). The superior catalytic activity was verified by the comparison with Bi NPs (Fig. 14h), and the stability was further evaluated through the test with 6 cycles (Fig. 14i). More recently, Wu et al. developed an N, P co-doped carbon catalyst with Bi anchored (Bi/NPC) for electrochemical \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) generation [95]. The synthesis route started from the construction of Bi containing ZIF-8 by self-assembly under ambient conditions, and the Bi/NPC hybrid was yielded after the subsequent pyrolysis and phosphorating. Intriguingly, the N-doped carbon substrate was further modified instead of the formation of BiP. With the synergistic effects of Bi catalytic sites, conductive carbon supports, and P-dopant proton suppliers, superior catalytic performance toward \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) synthesis was achieved with \(13.58\%\) FE at \(-0.4\:\mathrm{V}\) versus RHE.
The structural tunability and synergistic effect of the bimetal-supported heteroatom-doped carbon catalyst can improve the performance of NRR compared with the single metal-based catalyst. Using ZIF-8 as a precursor, electrorich metal sites can be obtained by thermolysis followed by acid etching. Ma et al. prepared an N-doped hollow carbon polyhedron supported by PdZn nanoparticles by etching, which can increase more electrons for vacancies in Pd-based catalysts and promote \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption, thus improving the performance of NRR [96]. The FE of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) reaches \(16.9\%\) , and compared with PdZn NP in buffer solution, the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield of the catalyst after etching is significantly higher \((5.28\,\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) \(\mathrm{mg_{cat.}}^{-1})\) . Furthermore, Cong et al. synthesized a CoRu@NC after annealing the \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}67\) that was generated through the ion-exchange reactions [97]. The \(\mathrm{CoRu}@\mathrm{NC}\) hybrids exhibited efficient electrocatalytic activity on NRR, providing an \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) production rate of \(56.82\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}\ \mathrm{mg_{cat.}}^{-1}\) with a \(2.02\%\) FE at \(-0.3\mathrm{~V~}\) versus RHE. In these works, the synergistic effect of bimetallic active sites was emphasized to be one of the vital factors for enhancing electrochemical \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) conversion.
Inspired by nitrogenase in nature, molybdenum and iron-based catalysts have drawn extensive attention in the field of NRR. The bimetallic NC nanomaterials from molybdenum or iron precursors show advanced characteristics that benefit the catalytic process, such as bimetallic active sites, synergistic effects, and excellent electrical conductivity. For instance, Zhang et al. prepared bimetallic nanoparticles of Mo-Co by pyrolysis of Mo-Co-ZIF-8 precursor. Compared with the single metal Co/NC, the catalytic activity and selectivity of bimetallic Mo-Co /NC are significantly enhanced, and the ammonia yield and FE are \(89.8\;\upmu\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{h}^{-1}\;\mathrm{g}_{\mathrm{cat.}}^{-1}\) and \(13.5\%\) , respectively [98]. In addition, under the continuous electrolysis of \(50000\;\mathrm{s}\) , the bimetallic Mo-Co /NC shows good electrochemical stability. Experimental studies have proved that pyridine nitrogen and pyrrole nitrogen are beneficial to NRR, to distinguish the ammonia generated from \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) rather than \(_\mathrm{N}\) in the precursor. Chen et al. synthesized a nitrogen-free catalyst and obtained porous microspheres of MoFe-PC by pyrolysis of phosphoric acid and bimetallic MOF, which not only included the bimetallic active site but also inherited the porous structure of the MOF precursor [99]. It is an efficient NRR catalyst with the highest ammonia yield of \(34.23~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat.}}^{-1}\) and FE of \(16.83\%\) at \(-0.5\;\mathrm{V}\) versus RHE. Cu-Mo bimetallic MOFs were also used as the precursors for fabricating electrocatalysts for \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\)
reduction. Furthermore, Ma et al. constructed a composite material with MOF-derived \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) NPs anchored on \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}\) nanoflowers \((\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2})\) and used it in the electrochemical \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) fixation as the catalyst [100]. The \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) NPs were synthesized through the calcination of Fe-MOF and subsequently confined by the in situ generated \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}\) nanoflowers. Compared to the \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}\) nanoflowers, enhanced NRR catalytic activity and stability were provided by the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}\) composite.
Wang et al. designed an electrocatalyst for coupling Fe and Mo as the active component, selected polyoxometalate-based MOF with polyvinylpyrrolidone \((\mathrm{PMo}_{12}@\mathrm{MOF-100(Fe)}@\mathrm{PVP})\) as the precursor, using their multicomponent and multi-interface structure to induce electron transfer and improve the electrical conductivity of the hybrid material (Fig. 15a) [101]. The authors reported for the first time a POMOFs-derived \(\mathrm{Fe_{1.89}M o_{4.11}O_{7}/F e S_{2}@C}\) catalyst with acidic potassium sulfate as an electrolytic solution, the FE of \(\mathrm{Fe_{1.89}M o_{4.11}O_{7}/F e S_{2}@C}\) is as high as \(54.7\%\) , with an \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield rate of \(105.3~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{mg_{cat.}}^{-1}\) (Fig. 15b, c). In another example, Duan and co-workers developed MoP NPs implanted in P-doped porous carbon \((\mathrm{MoP@PPC})\) from \(\mathrm{PMo}_{12}\) -based MOF (NENU-5) after high-temperature pyrolysis, oxidative etching, and phosphating (Fig. 15d) [102]. The Cu nodes of the original MOF were removed during the etching process, thus generating phosphatized Mo dispersed on the P-doped carbon with the octahedral morphology. Afterward, another POMOF-derived for \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) electrosynthesis was reported from the same group [103]. Based on the host–guest-assisted strategy, the nanostructured bimetallic sulfides, \(\mathrm{FeS}_{2}/\mathrm{MoS}_{2}@\mathrm{RGO}\) were synthesized through the reaction between thiourea and pre-fabricated \(\mathrm{PMo}_{12}@\mathrm{MOF}-\) \(100(\mathrm{Fe})@\mathrm{RGO}\) (Fig. 15e). Due to the integration of electrochemical characteristics of individual components, the hybrid material displayed excellent catalytic performance for NRR, with optimized \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield of \(41.1\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}\ \mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat.}}^{-1}\) and maximum FE of \(38.6\%\) at \(-0.2\mathrm{~V~}\) versus RHE (Fig. 15f). The sulfide composite showed sufficient catalytic activity and stability in both acid and alkaline electrolytes, presenting great application value.
Transition metal oxides (TMOs) are also promising substitutes for NRR because of their earth abundance and low price [104]. MOF-derived TMOs have attracted extensive attention in the field of catalysis. For early stage transition metals, the C-doped \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) \((\mathrm{C-Ti}_{x}\mathrm{O}_{y}/\mathrm{C})\) and C/Y-stabilized \(\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}\) derived from related MOFs were fabricated
for the electrochemical reduction of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) to \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) under mild conditions [105, 106]. For iron group elements, the MOF-derived \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NC}\) nanocomposites were reported by Luo et al. with high specific surface products and structural stability (Fig. 16a) [107]. \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) with oxygen vacancy \(\left(\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{o}}\right)\) was obtained by pyrolysis, and the synergistic effect with NC improved the performance of NRR. In 0.05 M \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\), it exhibits a high \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield of \(42.58~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat.}}^{-1}\) with \(8.5\%\) FE at \(-\,0.2\mathrm{~V~}\) versus RHE (Fig. 16b). During the 6 cycles of electrocatalysis, the \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NC}\) catalytic stability was ensured with neglectable fluctuation (Fig. 16c). Afterward, the MOF-derived hollow \(\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{NiO}@\mathrm{Ni}\) catalyst was developed by the same group, exhibiting excellent NRR performance in \(0.1\mathrm{~M~}\) KOH with a high \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield
of \(43.15~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat.}}^{-1}\) and FE of \(10.9\%\) at \(-\,0.7\mathrm{~V~}\) versus RHE [108]. Similarly, Wen and co-workers developed Zn-doped \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) nano-polyhedrons for the electrochemical \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) fixation (Fig. 16d) [109]. After low-temperature oxidation of the \(Z\mathrm{n}/\mathrm{Co}\) bimetallic ZIF precursors, the morphology of ZIF was retained with abundant simultaneously formed \(\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{o}}\) that acted as the Lewis acid sites (Fig. 16e). With the synergistic effect of \(\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{o}}\) and electron-rich Co sites, the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) transformation was promoted, offering an \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield of \(22.71~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat.}}^{-1}\) with an \(11.9\%\) FE (Fig. 16f). In another case, the promotive effect of \(\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{o}}\) for electrocatalysis was further verified by Cu-doped \(\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\) NPs on carbon nitride support \((\mathrm{CuCeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NC})\) [110]. Ye et al. fabricated the \(\mathrm{CuCeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NC}\) through the annealing
of melamine-cooperated CuCe-BTC under a reducing atmosphere (Fig. 16g, h). The maximum FE of \(34.6\%\) was provided with a high \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield of \(44.5~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat.}}^{-1}\) (Fig. 16i). Moreover, the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) fixation activity was further improved by introducing urea in the neutral electrolytic solution, using the MOF-derived \(\mathrm{CuO/Cu_{2}O@CD-CN/NiF}\) as the electrocatalyst [111]. According to these advances in MOF-derived TMO catalysts for NRR, the \(\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{o}}\) was claimed as the indispensable catalytic site.
In addition to TMO, other transition metal complexes (phosphide, sulfide) have also been widely used in electrocatalysis. For example, Wei et al. reported that ZIF67-derived cobalt disulfide nanoparticles supported in
In addition to TMO, other transition metal complexes (phosphide, sulfide) have also been widely used in electrocatalysis. For example, Wei et al. reported that ZIF-67-derived cobalt disulfide nanoparticles supported in nitrogen-doped carbon \((\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{NC})\) for \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) synthesis [112]. With excellent catalytic activity and electrical conductivity, \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{NC}\) catalyzed artificial \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) fixation provided a large \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield of \(17.45~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat.}}^{-1}\) and a high FE of \(4.6\%\) at \(-0.15\mathrm{~V~}\) versus RHE in \(0.1\mathrm{~M~}\) HCl. Furthermore, Liu et al. developed a highly selective \(\mathrm{FeNi}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}/\) NiS electrocatalyst prepared by electrodeposition of Fe/ Ni MOF-74 on carbon cloth as a precursor and curing at high temperatures [113]. The exposed crystal surface of the catalyst and the electronic structure of the d-band of the catalyst was adjusted by changing the stoichiometric ratio of Fe: Ni to enhance the selective adsorption of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\). Thus, heterogeneous interfaces with dual activity, which have
stronger NRR performance than single \(\mathrm{FeS}_{2}\) or NiS, exhibit ultra-high \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yields up to \(128.398\pm1.32~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) and a high FE of \(28.64\pm0.18\%\) at \(-0.30\,\mathrm{V}\) versus RHE in alkaline electrolytic solutions. In another case, Wu et al. synthesized NiCoS/C nanocages with enhanced chemical coupling using ZIF-67 as a precursor and applied them to the reduction of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) [114]. DFT calculations demonstrated that the strong coupling between C and NiCoS played an important role in reducing NRR overpotential and improving NRR selectivity. In \(0.1\mathrm{~M~Li}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\), the peak ammonia production reached \(58.5~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat}}^{-1}\).
For metal phosphides, Guo et al. prepared a novel MOF of cobalt phosphide hollow nanocages assembled from CoP nanosheets [115]. This nanoparticle-nanosheet-nanocages hierarchical structure provides abundant active sites for NRR, with an FE of \(7.36\%\) at \(0\,\mathrm{~V~}\) and maximum \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yields of \(10.78~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat}}^{-1}\) at \(-0.4\;\mathrm{V}\) versus RHE. Li and co-authors reported a nitrogen-doped carbon nanosheet embedded with \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{P}\) clusters \((\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{P/NC})\) for the electrochemical fixation of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) [116]. The hybrid was synthesized through a low-temperature pyrolysis-phosphating strategy, with Cu-MOF precursors. Benefiting from the N-dopants that facilitated the electrical conductivity and defect formation as well as the phosphate that tuned the d-band of \(\mathrm{Cu}\), the generation of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) was achieved with a 10.4 yield rate and \(6.3\%\) FE at \(-0.1\;\mathrm{V}\) versus RHE. As a result, the green and efficient electrochemical NRR to \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) products were further enriched with these advances in MOF-derived metal phosphides.
In all, MOF-derived nanostructures and their hybrids were able to be fabricated rationally and precisely with the merits of adjustable structures, abundant compositions, and diverse morphologies originating from the MOF precursors. To date, these materials are undoubtedly one of the promising candidates for electrocatalytic \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) synthesis under mild conditions. The related catalysts and their performance are listed in Table 2.
## 4  MOF‑Related Electrocatalysts for Nitrate Reduction Reactions
To kill two birds with one stone, the electrochemical reduction of nitrate \(\left(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{\mathrm{~-~}}\right)\) is considered a promising strategy to remove \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{\mathrm{~-~}}\) -containing pollutants in water with the simultaneous \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) synthesis under ambient conditions, which is
also called electrochemical denitration [117, 118]. Although extensive investigations have been reported in this field, there are few examples were realized using MOF-based electrocatalysts under mild conditions. Due to the complexity of the plausible mechanism of electrochemical denitration, several advanced works were presented yielding \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) as the major product. For example, Sun and co-workers proposed an electrochemical conversion of \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{\mathrm{~-~}}\) to \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\), adopting a Fe–Ni bimetallic MOF-derived nanomaterial as the effective electrocatalyst [119]. Theoretically, further reduction and protonation are essential for the more valuable \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) product. According to the discussion about the NRR process in the previous section, with porous structure and intrinsic hydrophobicity, MOFs were also presumed to have the ability to exhibit their superior catalytic activity and product selectivity for electrochemical conversion of \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{\mathrm{~-~}}\) to \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) process under ambient conditions. More recently, the electrochemical conversion of \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{\mathrm{~-~}}\) to \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) was extensively explored by MOF-related catalysts, and recent advances were listed as follows.
## 4.1  MOF‑Based Electrocatalysts
Similar to the MOF-based electrocatalysts for NRR, the precisely designed and delicately synthesized pristine MOFs or MOF-hybrids with proper hydrophobicity, sufficient conductivity, and robust catalytic active sites can tune the \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{\mathrm{~-~}}\) reduction for the harvest of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) products. For example, Lv et al. reported 2D In-MOF electrocatalysts with atomically dispersed active sites, high electron, and proton transport, and confined microporous environments for electrochemical denitration (Fig. 17a) [120]. After a thorough exploration of the catalysts and reaction conditions, the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) product was provided with the highest \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield rate of \(92.98\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{h}^{-1}\;\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat}}^{-1}\) \(\mathrm{pH}\!=\!2\!\!\) and optimal FE of \(56.57\%\) \(\mathrm{pH}\!=\!3\!\!\) ) at \(-0.7\mathrm{~V~}\) versus RHE. Notably, the competing HER process can be tuned by the \(\mathrm{pH}\) value of the electrolytic solution (Fig. 17b). With proper \(\mathrm{pH}\) value, the metal active sites were properly exposed with appropriate ligand dissociation, while excessive ligand dissociation at low \(\mathrm{pH}\) led to increasing water splitting (Fig. 17c) and no ligand dissociation at high \(\mathrm{pH}\) caused complete cessation of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) generation (Fig. 17d). In another case that adopted 2D MOF, Zhu et al. developed a Cu cluster-modified
conductive Cu-MOF composite electrocatalysts through in situ synthetic strategy for electrochemical reduction of \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{\mathrm{~-~}}\) [121]. During the electrocatalysis, the pre-fabricated Cu-HHTP nanorods were partially transformed into metallic Cu clusters confined internally. In \(0.5\:\mathrm{M}\:\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) , \(85.81\%\) of \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{\mathrm{~-~}}\) was converted into \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) with \(96.84\%\) selectivity, a yield rate of \(1.84\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{h}^{-1}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) , and an FE of \(67.55\%\) . According to the DFT calculation, the metallic Cu filling inside MOF was identified as a catalytic active site and the catalytic performance was improved due to the facilitation of electron transfer by the \(\mathrm{Cu}(111)\) crystal face. Furthermore, Jiang and co-workers fabricated a series of noble metal nanodots (NDs) encapsulated in \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOFs (M-NDs/Zr-MOF, \(\mathbf{M}\!=\!\mathbf{P}\mathbf{d}\) , Ag, or Au) and applied them in the \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\mathrm{RR}\) process as the electrocatalysts (Fig. 17e-h) [122]. The \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{\mathrm{~-~}}\) to \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) catalytic activity was promoted by the confined metal NDs, while the conductivity and mass
transport was facilitated with uniform-sized pores and redox-reversible tetrathiafulvalene (TTF) motifs inside the \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOF skeleton. In \(0.1\mathrm{~M~Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) with \(500\;\mathrm{ppm}\;\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{\mathrm{~-~}}\) , the highest \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield was obtained as \(287.31\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}\) \(\mathrm{mg_{cat.}}^{-1}\) with \(58.1\%\) FE at \(-1.3\mathrm{~V~}\) versus RHE with Pd- \(\mathrm{NDs/Zr-MOF}\) as the electrocatalyst (Fig. 17i, j). In another investigation using noble-metal-related MOF-hybrid electrocatalysts, Qin and co-workers reported the preparation of a \(\mathrm{Ru}_{x}\mathrm{O}_{y}\) mosaic Ni-MOF composite (RuNi-MOF) and its application in electrochemical \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\mathrm{RR}\) [123]. Through solvothermal strategy, a nearly \(100\%\) production selectivity for \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) was realized, with a maximum yield of up to \(274~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat.}}^{-1}\) and FE of \(\sim\!73\%\) at \(-1.7\) and \(-1.2\mathrm{~V~}\) versus \(\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}\) , respectively. According to the theoretical calculation, the \(\mathrm{Ru}_{3}\) site was indicated as the main active center for the generation of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) , undergoing a direct electron-mediated pathway.
## 4.2 MOF-Derived Electrocatalysts
4.2 MOF-Derived ElectrocatalystsA pioneer work was reported by Li et al. during the investigation of the electrosynthesis of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) from \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}\)catalyzed by metal nanoclusters [124]. The authors prepared a series of pristine MOFs (Zn, Cu, Bi, Fe, and Co MOFs), MOF-derived NPC, MOF-derived SACs (Fe, Co, and Ni), and MOF-derived Co NPs/NPC and applied them in the electrochemical denitration. After extensive measurements, moderated catalytic performance was achieved in each case compared to that of strained Ru nanoclusters. Considering the relatively low cost of non-precious metal-based MOFs, this work inspired further exploration of the possibility of MOF-derived electrocatalysts in the \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}\)-to-\(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) transformation process. Meanwhile, Zhu et al. developed a Cu-N-C electrocatalyst by pyrolysis of a modified Cu-MOF structure for the electrocatalytic denitration process [125]. The highly dispersed active Cu sites exhibited superior catalytic ability in the reduction of \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}\), alleviating the release of \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-}\)during the reduction reaction. In 2022, Liu and colleagues developed bimetallic MOF-derived nitrogen-doped carbon materials equipped with CuNi alloy nanoparticles at the electrocatalysts for \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\mathrm{RR}\) [126]. With CuNi-BTC precursors prepared by the solvothermal method, a series of CuNi/NC with different Cu-Ni ratios were fabricated through pyrolysis. Inherited from the MOFs, the CuNi/NC catalysts maintained
the octahedral morphology with the CuNi alloy particles enveloped in the nitrogen-doped carbon matrix (Fig. 18a). The conversion of \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}\)was higher than \(80\%\) with \(\mathrm{Cu/NC}\) and decreased with the addition of Ni, while the values of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) selectivity and FE reached a peak at Cu: \(\mathrm{Ni}=5:1\), obtaining \(94.4\%\) and \(79.6\%\), respectively (Fig. 18b-d). A possible proton-mediated synthetic route of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) was proposed due to the synergistic effect of Cu-Ni for facilitating electron and proton transfer. Furthermore, Zhang et al. proposed a MOF-74-derived Co-doped \(\mathrm{Fe}@\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) electrocatalyst for \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\mathrm{RR}\) (Fig. 18e) [127]. According to the experimental and computational results, the d-band of the Fe-center was modulated by the Co dopants, thus tuning the adsorption energy of intermediates and suppressing the HER process. The enhanced \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) generation was shown with a maximum yield of \(1505.9\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) at \(-0.95\) versus RHE and \(95.7\%\ \mathrm{NH}_{3}\) selectivity as well as FE of \(85.8\%\) at \(-0.75\ \mathrm{V}\) versus RHE in \(0.1\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) (Fig. 18f, g).
The study of MOF-related catalysts for electrochemical denitration is still in its infancy [128]. The inspiring works originated from the electrocatalytic reduction process of other molecules, such as carbon dioxide, oxygen, and nitrogen. It is attractive and challenging the investigation of precise design and synthesis of MOF-based or MOF-derived electrocatalysts with efficient catalytic activity and superior product selectivity. Moreover, since \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) is valuable in
modern industrial civilization and the possible mechanism of \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\mathrm{RR}\) is complicated, further exploration of the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) synthetic pathway with theoretical calculation and in situ characterization is essential for the guidance of practical use.
## 5 Conclusion and Perspective
In this review, we have summarized recent works focusing on the development of effective and efficient MOF electrocatalysts for electrochemical \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) synthesis. A timeline since 2017 for selected works towards electrosynthesis of ammonia is shown in Fig. 19. According to our analysis of several recent studies, the electrocatalytic performance is determined by the ability of the superficial adsorption, reductive activation, and competitive \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) selectivity that can be tuned by proper nanoengineering of the electrocatalysts. The electronic structure and morphology variation of the catalysts plays vital roles in achieving enhanced catalytic capability and superior product selectivity. Hence, fabricating electrocatalysts from MOFs indeed facilitates these electrochemical processes as practical and effective replacements for current industrial processes in synthetic and energy aspects. However, there are still drawbacks that are significant and challenging. First, the development of novel electrocatalysts is still dependent on experience or trial-and-error procedures, which costs a lot of time and energy. Second, the synthetic process and modification
strategy often require harsh conditions to obtain the optimized morphology and boost catalytic performance, and reproducibility can be poor. Third, the surface environment may change under catalytic conditions, which can be difficult to predict and detect. Moreover, the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) with its intrinsic insolubility and inertness requires extensive exploration to promote the reactivity, while the electrochemical conversion of nitrogen oxides is mainly valuable in waste gas and water treatment which is a drop in the ocean for modern ammonia needs. Therefore, some advanced techniques and methods are required to assist in the fabrication of high-performance MOF-related catalysts for \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) synthesis in the future, as discussed below.
1) Machine learning (ML) is one of the significant breakthroughs in computer science these days. In the study of chemistry and material science, ML was adopted as a powerful tool to reasonably predict new molecules or materials for specific applications [129, 130]. With the help of an established database of nanomaterials, the proper candidates of electrocatalysts for ammonia production can be estimated before experimental corroboration. As a result, it should be feasible to develop functional \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) synthesis catalysts from MOFs with the help of ML.
2) Mild synthetic processing is one of the ultimate pursuits in material science. Most of the current synthetic protocols for MOF-derived electrocatalysts in \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) synthesis still require harsh conditions, excessive synthesis time and steps, and limited precursor types. Moreover,
the existing synthetic protocols of robust MOF-related electrocatalysts are far from large-scale industrial applications. Novel strategies with simple manipulation and mild conditions are under investigation in the field of electrocatalysis [131]. Therefore, the development of new synthetic routes using new reaction precursors under mild conditions is important for the sustainable application of electrochemical \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) production.
3) In situ characterization is a useful tool for understanding specific reaction mechanisms. Due to the complex and diverse reaction processes in the synthesis of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) from \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}\), in situ characterization should be helpful for the exploration of the reaction mechanism in detail. Through the in situ characterization, the key intermediates can be confirmed, the catalytic process can be understood, the conformational relationships can be clarified, and the synthesis of catalysts can be further guided. Thus, the development of MOF-related electrocatalysts with efficient and active catalytic performance toward the synthesis of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) can be extensively improved.
4) Conversion of other \(N\)-containing substrates is also an attractive research field in the electrosynthesis of ammonia. As nitrogen element is fully oxidized in \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}\)ion, other nitrogen oxides with relatively lower oxidation states have a high probability of being reduced to ammonia. There are existing developments of metal-based nanomaterials with extraordinary catalytic performance in the conversion of \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-}\) [132–136] and prediction of 2D MOF-catalyzed electrochemical conversion of \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) through DFT calculation [137]. The MOF-based and derived materials are very promising catalysts for promoting the conversion of these \(\mathrm{NO}_{x}\) particles or ions into \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\).
In all, the investigation of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) electrosynthesis under mild conditions is still at a preliminary stage and far from meeting the actual needs [138]. Developing comprehensive performance and low-cost electrocatalysts is no doubt the most promising outlet for constructing \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) electrosynthesis that can complement or even replace the existing industrial processes. We hope that this review is able to offer inspiration for the rational development of MOF-related electrocatalysts for \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) synthesis that would also make significant contributions and breakthroughs in materials science, synthetic chemistry, catalysis, and other fields.
